{
  "result": "Every morning, the city rehearses its arrival. Shopkeepers lift shutters, buses sigh at corners, and windows brighten one by one. Yet the most interesting part of the morning is not its noise. It is the brief moment before anyone seems entirely sure what the day will become for us all.\n\nA baker stands in a doorway, holding a tray of bread. A cyclist pauses to check a loose shoelace. Across the street, a child points at a pigeon with the astonishment usually reserved for dragons. Nobody is performing for an audience, yet each person briefly belongs to everyone else\u2019s story.\n\nWe often imagine meaningful lives as a sequence of major decisions: choosing a career, moving away, falling in love. But much of a life is made from smaller gestures: holding a door, remembering a name, making room on a crowded bench. These actions rarely announce themselves as important at first.\n\nPerhaps that is why a familiar place can become precious without our noticing. The corner caf\u00e9 is not remarkable because its chairs match or its coffee wins awards. It matters because someone there remembers how you take it, and asks whether your mother is feeling better after her recent illness.\n\nTomorrow, the city will rehearse again. Some windows will stay dark, and new faces will appear at the bus stop. The pigeon may return, indifferent to its admirers. If we pay attention, we might notice how much of the world is quietly held together by ordinary acts of human care.",
